```

Your **“add years”** function is losing the day because it currently does this:

- `IncreaseDateByXYears(XYears)` → calls `IncreaseDateByXMonths(12 * XYears)`
- adding **month-by-month** can pass through **February**
- when the date is the 29th/30th/31st, February may not have that day → you clamp down to **28**
- after that, you never “restore” back to 29, so you end up with **28** permanently

That’s why you got **28/9/2034** instead of **29/9/2034**.

## Fix
For “add years”, don’t add 12×months. Just add to the year directly and clamp the day only if it becomes invalid (this matches your “faster” function logic).

<llm-snippet-file>Project.cpp</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// ... existing code ...

sDate IncreaseDateByXYears (short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    // Add years directly to avoid drifting when passing through February
    Date.Year += XYears;

    short lastDayInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > lastDayInTargetMonth)
    {
        Date.Day = lastDayInTargetMonth;
    }

    return Date;
}

// ... existing code ...

sDate IncreaseDateByXYearsFaster (short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    Date.Year += XYears;

    short lastDayInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > lastDayInTargetMonth)
        Date.Day = lastDayInTargetMonth;
    return Date;
}

// ... existing code ...
```

### What this changes for your example
From **29/9/2024 + 10 years**:
- September always has 30 days, so **29 stays valid**
- result becomes **29/9/2034** (no unwanted drop to 28)

If you want, I can also help you rename/remove the duplicated logic between `IncreaseDateByXYears` and `IncreaseDateByXYearsFaster` to keep just one clean function.

Your **“add years”** function is losing the day because it currently does this:

- `IncreaseDateByXYears(XYears)` → calls `IncreaseDateByXMonths(12 * XYears)`
- adding **month-by-month** can pass through **February**
- when the date is the 29th/30th/31st, February may not have that day → you clamp down to **28**
- after that, you never “restore” back to 29, so you end up with **28** permanently

That’s why you got **28/9/2034** instead of **29/9/2034**.

## Fix
For “add years”, don’t add 12×months. Just add to the year directly and clamp the day only if it becomes invalid (this matches your “faster” function logic).

<llm-snippet-file>Project.cpp</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// ... existing code ...

sDate IncreaseDateByXYears (short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    // Add years directly to avoid drifting when passing through February
    Date.Year += XYears;

    short lastDayInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > lastDayInTargetMonth)
    {
        Date.Day = lastDayInTargetMonth;
    }

    return Date;
}

// ... existing code ...

sDate IncreaseDateByXYearsFaster (short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    Date.Year += XYears;

    short lastDayInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > lastDayInTargetMonth)
        Date.Day = lastDayInTargetMonth;
    return Date;
}

// ... existing code ...
```

### What this changes for your example
From **29/9/2024 + 10 years**:
- September always has 30 days, so **29 stays valid**
- result becomes **29/9/2034** (no unwanted drop to 28)

