What’s still improvable:

- **Performance refactoring (Replace loop with arithmetic):** `IncreaseDateByXMonths` is currently **O(monthsToAdd)** because it calls “+1 month” repeatedly. You can refactor it to **O(1)** by converting `(year, month)` to a “total months” number, adding, then converting back.
- **Change signature / robustness:** using `short` for counts like `monthsToAdd` is restrictive (and awkward if you ever pass big values). Prefer `int` (or at least handle `monthsToAdd <= 0` explicitly).
- **Readability:** the month/year rollover logic becomes clearer when centralized in the O(1) function; the “clamp day to end-of-month” rule remains the same.

Here is a cleaner, faster refactoring (still uses your existing `NumberOfDaysInAMonth` and preserves behavior for valid inputs):

```cpp
sDate IncreaseDateByOneMonth(sDate date)
{
    if (date.Month == 12)
    {
        date.Month = 1;
        ++date.Year;
    }
    else
    {
        ++date.Month;
    }

    const short daysInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(date.Month, date.Year);
    if (date.Day > daysInTargetMonth)
        date.Day = daysInTargetMonth;

    return date;
}

sDate IncreaseDateByXMonths(int monthsToAdd, sDate date)
{
    if (monthsToAdd <= 0)
        return date;

    // Convert to a 0-based "absolute month index", add months, convert back.
    const int startMonthIndex = static_cast<int>(date.Year) * 12 + (static_cast<int>(date.Month) - 1);
    const int targetMonthIndex = startMonthIndex + monthsToAdd;

    date.Year  = static_cast<short>(targetMonthIndex / 12);
    date.Month = static_cast<short>((targetMonthIndex % 12) + 1);

    const short daysInTargetMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(date.Month, date.Year);
    if (date.Day > daysInTargetMonth)
        date.Day = daysInTargetMonth;

    return date;
}
```

