The “fix” should live in **`IncreaseDateByOneYear`** (and any other function that changes month/year in a way that could make the day invalid).

Right now your `IncreaseDateByOneYear` likely does something like:

- increment `Year`
- **does not** adjust `Day`

So `29/02/2020 + 1 year` becomes `29/02/2021` (invalid), unless you **clamp** the day to the last valid day in February.

A clean way is to **Extract Function**: make a small helper that clamps the day after any change to month/year, and call it from `IncreaseDateByOneMonth`, `IncreaseDateByOneYear`, and the “faster” year/month adders.

```cpp
static sDate ClampDayToMonth(sDate date)
{
    const short daysInMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(date.Month, date.Year);
    if (date.Day > daysInMonth)
        date.Day = daysInMonth;
    return date;
}

sDate IncreaseDateByOneYear(sDate date)
{
    ++date.Year;
    return ClampDayToMonth(date); // fixes 29/2 -> 28/2 on non-leap years
}
```

After this, calling `IncreaseDateByOneYear({29, 2, 2020})` will correctly produce **`28/2/2021`**.

