That solution is **almost correct**, and the overall idea is good:  
- If it’s the **1st day**, you jump to the **last day of the previous month**.  
- Otherwise, just decrement the day.

### The main issue
When `Date.Month == 1` (January), you do:

```cpp
Date.Month = 12;
Date.Day = 31;
Date.Year--;
```

This assumes the previous month is **always** December 31—which is true (December always has 31 days). So for this specific branch, it’s **fine**.

### The improvement (recommended)
To make the function more consistent and less “special-cased”, set the year/month first, then compute the correct last day using `NumberOfDaysInAMonth()` in *both* branches. That way if you ever change month handling, you don’t have a hard-coded `31` sitting around.

```cpp
stDate DecreaseDateByOneDay(stDate Date)
{
    if (Date.Day == 1)
    {
        if (Date.Month == 1)
        {
            Date.Month = 12;
            Date.Year--;
        }
        else
        {
            Date.Month--;
        }

        Date.Day = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    }
    else
    {
        Date.Day--;
    }
    return Date;
}
```

### One more practical note
Make sure your type name matches your code (`stDate` vs `sDate`). In your project it’s `sDate`, so use that to avoid a compile error.

