Yes — a more portable C++ version is to use a normal member function pair, or better, a small wrapper pattern.

## Portable version

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class clsPerson
{
private:
    std::string _FirstName;

public:
    void SetFirstName(const std::string& FirstName)
    {
        _FirstName = FirstName;
    }

    const std::string& GetFirstName() const
    {
        return _FirstName;
    }
};

int main()
{
    clsPerson Person1;

    Person1.SetFirstName("Mohammed");
    std::cout << Person1.GetFirstName() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

## Why this is better

- **Works in standard C++**: no compiler-specific property syntax.
- **`const` getter**: safer and more correct.
- **No unnecessary copy in setter**: uses `const std::string&`.
- **Cleaner for cross-platform projects**.

## If you want property-like syntax
In standard C++, you usually simulate it with methods, not true properties. So instead of:

```cpp
Person1.FirstName = "Mohammed";
```

you use:

```cpp
Person1.SetFirstName("Mohammed");
```

If you want, I can also show a **cleaner class design using `get/set` naming**, or a **`struct`/`class` version with validation**.

