The ambiguity happens here:

```cpp
IsNumberBetween(Number, From, To)
```

because the argument types are:

```cpp
float, double, double
```

Your overloads include both:

```cpp
static bool IsNumberBetween(float Number, float from, float to)
static bool IsNumberBetween(double Number, double From, double To)
```

For this call, C++ can either:

- convert `From` and `To` from `double` to `float`, matching the `float` overload
- convert `Number` from `float` to `double`, matching the `double` overload

Neither is clearly better, so the call is ambiguous.

## Best fix

In `ReadFloatNumberBetween`, keep everything consistently `float`.

<llm-snippet-file>clsInputValidate.h</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
// ... existing code ...

static double ReadFloatNumber(string ErrorMessage = "Invalid Number, Enter again\n")
{
    float Number;
    while (!(cin >> Number)) {
        cin.clear();
        cin.ignore(numeric_limits<streamsize>::max(), '\n');
        cout << ErrorMessage;
    }
    return Number;
}

static float ReadFloatNumberBetween(float From, float To, string ErrorMessage = "Number is not within range, Enter again:\n")
{
    float Number = ReadFloatNumber();

    while (!IsNumberBetween(Number, From, To)) {
        cout << ErrorMessage;
        Number = ReadFloatNumber();
    }
    return Number;
}

static double ReadDblNumber(string ErrorMessage = "Invalid Number, Enter again\n")
{
    double Number;

// ... existing code ...
```

Also, I recommend changing `ReadFloatNumber` to return `float`, because right now it reads a `float` but returns `double`.

<llm-snippet-file>clsInputValidate.h</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
// ... existing code ...

static float ReadFloatNumber(string ErrorMessage = "Invalid Number, Enter again\n")
{
    float Number;
    while (!(cin >> Number)) {
        cin.clear();
        cin.ignore(numeric_limits<streamsize>::max(), '\n');
        cout << ErrorMessage;
    }
    return Number;
}

static float ReadFloatNumberBetween(float From, float To, string ErrorMessage = "Number is not within range, Enter again:\n")
{
    float Number = ReadFloatNumber();

    while (!IsNumberBetween(Number, From, To)) {
        cout << ErrorMessage;
        Number = ReadFloatNumber();
    }
    return Number;
}

static double ReadDblNumber(string ErrorMessage = "Invalid Number, Enter again\n")
{
    double Number;

// ... existing code ...
```

## Small bug in your current code

Inside `ReadFloatNumberBetween`, you currently have:

```cpp
Number = ReadDblNumber();
```

That should be:

```cpp
Number = ReadFloatNumber();
```

Otherwise, if the user enters an out-of-range float, you start reading a double instead.

## Alternative quick fix

If you don’t want to change function signatures, you can force the `double` overload:

```cpp
while (!IsNumberBetween(static_cast<double>(Number), From, To)) {
```

But the cleaner solution is to keep the float function fully `float`, and the double function fully `double`.

The ambiguity happens here:

```cpp
IsNumberBetween(Number, From, To)
```

because the argument types are:

```cpp
float, double, double
```

Your overloads include both:

```cpp
static bool IsNumberBetween(float Number, float from, float to)
static bool IsNumberBetween(double Number, double From, double To)
```

For this call, C++ can either:

- convert `From` and `To` from `double` to `float`, matching the `float` overload
- convert `Number` from `float` to `double`, matching the `double` overload

