Not really — it works on your Linux terminal, but it’s **not the best way** in C++.

You currently have something like:

```cpp
system("read -p 'Press Enter to continue...' var");
```

### Why it’s not ideal

`system()` has a few downsides:

- It depends on the shell/OS. This works on Linux/macOS shells, but not on Windows.
- It is slower because it starts a shell process.
- It can be risky if dynamic input is ever passed into it.
- It makes the program less “pure C++”.

For a C++ console app, prefer using standard input.

### Better C++14-friendly approach

Use `cin.ignore()` and `cin.get()`:

```cpp
cout << setw(37) << left << "" << "\n\tPress Enter to go back to Main Menue...\n";

cin.ignore(numeric_limits<streamsize>::max(), '\n');
cin.get();
```

You’ll also need:

```cpp
#include <limits>
```

### Suggested edit

<llm-snippet-file>clsMainScreen.h</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
#pragma once
#include <iostream>
#include "clsScreen.h"
#include "clsInputValidate.h"
#include <iomanip>
#include <limits>

// ... existing code ...

       static  void _GoBackToMainMenue()
        {
            cout << setw(37) << left << ""<<"\n\tPress Enter to go back to Main Menue...\n";

            cin.ignore(numeric_limits<streamsize>::max(), '\n');
            cin.get();

            ShowMainMenue();
        }

// ... existing code ...
```

### Small note

Your message says **“Press any key”**, but the command actually waits for **Enter**, not any key. Standard C++ cannot easily detect “any key” without platform-specific libraries, so saying **“Press Enter”** is more accurate.

So the short answer: **use `cin.ignore()` + `cin.get()` instead of `system("read ...")`.**

