        cout << setw(37) << left << "" << "\t[5] Find User.\n";
        cout << setw(37) << left << "" << "\t[6] Main Menue.\n";
        cout << setw(37) << left << "" << "===========================================\n";

        _PerformManageUsersMenueOption((enManageUsersMenueOptions)ReadManageUsersMenueOption());
    }
};
#endif //PROJECT1L4BANKEXTENSION2_CLSMANAGEUSERSSCREEN_H
```

Because `_ShowListUsersScreen()` is acting as a **wrapper/helper function** around:

```cpp
clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();
```

So the flow is:

```cpp
case enManageUsersMenueOptions::eListUsers:
{
    clsScreen::_ClearScreen();
    _ShowListUsersScreen();
    _GoBackToManageUsersMenue();
    break;
}
```

And `_ShowListUsersScreen()` itself does:

```cpp
static void _ShowListUsersScreen()
{
    clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();
}
```

So yes — technically, you **could** call it directly:

```cpp
case enManageUsersMenueOptions::eListUsers:
{
    clsScreen::_ClearScreen();
    clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();
    _GoBackToManageUsersMenue();
    break;
}
```

But using `_ShowListUsersScreen()` has some benefits:

1. **Consistency**

   All menu options follow the same pattern:

```cpp
_ShowListUsersScreen();
_ShowAddNewUserScreen();
_ShowDeleteUserScreen();
_ShowUpdateUserScreen();
_ShowFindUserScreen();
```

That makes the switch cleaner and easier to read.

2. **Future flexibility**

   Today `_ShowListUsersScreen()` only calls:

```cpp
clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();
```

But later you may want to add extra logic:

```cpp
static void _ShowListUsersScreen()
{
    // check permissions
    // log action
    // prepare data
    clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();
}
```

Then you only modify one helper function, not the switch logic.

3. **Separation of responsibilities**

   The switch decides **which option was chosen**.

   The helper function decides **how to show that screen**.

So the short answer is:

> We don’t go directly to `clsListUsersScreen::ShowUsersList();` mainly to keep the menu code cleaner, consistent, and easier to extend later.

In this small case, both are valid. The wrapper is more about good organization than necessity.

