Use **`Set`**, not `Get`.

A **getter** should only read/return the current value:

```cpp
string GetFirstName();
```

A **setter** should receive the new value and store it:

```cpp
void SetFirstName(string FirstName);
```

So this is incorrect:

```cpp
User.SetFirstName() = clsInputValidate::ReadString();
```

Because `SetFirstName` expects **1 argument**, but you are calling it with **0 arguments**.

Use this instead:

```cpp
User.SetFirstName(clsInputValidate::ReadString());
```

Same idea for the others:

```cpp
User.SetLastName(clsInputValidate::ReadString());
User.SetEmail(clsInputValidate::ReadString());
User.SetPhone(clsInputValidate::ReadString());
User.SetPassword(clsInputValidate::ReadString());
User.SetPermissions(_ReadPermissionsToSet());
```

So your input section should be:

```cpp
cout << "\nEnter FirstName: ";
User.SetFirstName(clsInputValidate::ReadString());

cout << "\nEnter LastName: ";
User.SetLastName(clsInputValidate::ReadString());

cout << "\nEnter Email: ";
User.SetEmail(clsInputValidate::ReadString());

cout << "\nEnter Phone: ";
User.SetPhone(clsInputValidate::ReadString());

cout << "\nEnter Password: ";
User.SetPassword(clsInputValidate::ReadString());

cout << "\nEnter Permission: ";
User.SetPermissions(_ReadPermissionsToSet());
```

In short:

- **Use `Set...(...)` when assigning/updating**
- **Use `Get...()` when reading/displaying**

