```

The issue is caused by these two lines:

```c++
cout << "\nUser: " << CurrentUser;
cout << "\nDate: " << clsDate();
```

`cout << ...` only works automatically for built-in types like `int`, `double`, `string`, `char*`, etc.

But `CurrentUser` is an object of type `clsUser`, and `clsDate()` creates an object of type `clsDate`. C++ does **not** know how to print those custom classes unless you tell it how.

So CLion is saying:

> “I found `operator<<` for numbers, strings, bools, manipulators like `endl`, etc., but none that accepts `clsUser` or `clsDate`.”

---

## Recommended solution

Print a specific string/value from those objects instead of the whole object.

For example, if `clsUser` has something like `UserName`, `UserName()`, `GetUserName()`, or similar, use that:

<llm-snippet-file>include/Screens/clsScreen.h</llm-snippet-file>
```c++
        static void _DrawScreenHeader(string Title, string SubTitle = "")
        {
            cout << "\t\t\t\t\t______________________________________";
            cout << "\n\n\t\t\t\t\t  " << Title;

            if (SubTitle != "")
            {
                cout << "\n\t\t\t\t\t  " << SubTitle;
            }
            cout << "\n\t\t\t\t\t______________________________________\n\n";

            cout << "\nUser: " << CurrentUser.UserName;
            cout << "\nDate: " << clsDate().DateToString();
        }
```

The exact member/function names may differ in your project. Use whatever your classes already provide, for example:

```c++
cout << "\nUser: " << CurrentUser.GetUserName();
cout << "\nDate: " << clsDate().ToString();
```

or:

```c++
cout << "\nUser: " << CurrentUser.UserName();
cout << "\nDate: " << clsDate().DateToString();
```

---

## Alternative solution: overload `operator<<`

If you want this syntax to work:

```c++
cout << CurrentUser;
cout << clsDate();
```

then define stream insertion operators for your classes.

For `clsDate`, you could add something like:

```c++
ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const clsDate& date)
{
    os << date.DateToString();
    return os;
}
```

For `clsUser`, something like:

```c++
ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const clsUser& user)
{
    os << user.UserName;
    return os;
}
```

However, this requires knowing the actual public methods or fields available in `clsDate` and `clsUser`.

---

## Best practical fix here

Since this is only for the screen header, the simplest and cleanest fix is:

```c++
cout << "\nUser: " << CurrentUser.GetUserName();
cout << "\nDate: " << clsDate().DateToString();
```

Use the equivalent methods that exist in your classes.

In short: **don’t stream the whole object unless you overload `operator<<`; stream one of its printable properties instead.**

