Yes, you **can replace `void` with `bool`**, and that is actually a good solution **if you want `Withdraw()` itself to decide whether the withdrawal succeeded or failed**.

That means your code here can stay like this:

```c++
if (Client1.Withdraw(Amount))
```

But you must change the `Withdraw` function inside `clsBankClient`.

## What to change

Find the `Withdraw` function in `clsBankClient.h` or `clsBankClient.cpp`.

It is probably currently something like:

```c++
void Withdraw(double Amount)
{
    AccountBalance -= Amount;
    Save();
}
```

Change it to return `bool`:

<llm-snippet-file>clsBankClient.h</llm-snippet-file>
```c++
    bool Withdraw(double Amount)
    {
        if (Amount > _AccountBalance)
        {
            return false;
        }

        _AccountBalance -= Amount;
        Save();

        return true;
    }
```

Or, if your function uses `GetAccountBalance()` and `SetAccountBalance()`, it may look like this:

<llm-snippet-file>clsBankClient.h</llm-snippet-file>
```c++
    bool Withdraw(double Amount)
    {
        if (Amount > GetAccountBalance())
        {
            return false;
        }

        SetAccountBalance(GetAccountBalance() - Amount);
        Save();

        return true;
    }
```

