## Important note

If you change `Withdraw` from:

```c++
void Withdraw(double Amount)
```

to:

```c++
bool Withdraw(double Amount)
```

then every place that calls `Withdraw()` will still work, even if it ignores the returned value:

```c++
Client1.Withdraw(Amount);
```

That is allowed in C++.

So yes, changing `void` to `bool` is a clean solution.

The **best solution is to make `Withdraw()` return `bool`**.

Why? Because the withdrawal operation itself should know whether it succeeded or failed.

## Best design

`Withdraw()` should:

1. Check if the amount is greater than the balance.
2. If yes, return `false`.
3. If no, subtract the amount.
4. Save the updated client.
5. Return `true`.

So the function should be like:

<llm-snippet-file>clsBankClient.h</llm-snippet-file>
```c++
    bool Withdraw(double Amount)
    {
        if (Amount > _AccountBalance)
        {
            return false;
        }

        _AccountBalance -= Amount;
        Save();

        return true;
    }
```

Then your screen code can correctly do:

```c++
if (Client1.Withdraw(Amount))
{
    cout << "\nAmount Withdrew Successfully.\n";
}
else
{
    cout << "\nCannot withdraw, Insufficient Balance!\n";
}
```

## Why this is better than checking balance in the screen

This is okay:

```c++
if (Amount <= Client1.GetAccountBalance())
{
    Client1.Withdraw(Amount);
}
```

But it is **not the best design**, because now the screen is responsible for withdrawal rules.

For example, if later you withdraw money from another place in the program, you might forget to check the balance there.

Better:

```c++
Client1.Withdraw(Amount)
```

The rule is inside the client class, so every part of the program uses the same safe logic.

## Final answer

Use this design:

```c++
bool Withdraw(double Amount)
```

instead of:

```c++
void Withdraw(double Amount)
```

It is cleaner, safer, and easier to reuse.

The **best solution is to make `Withdraw()` return `bool`**.

Why? Because the withdrawal operation itself should know whether it succeeded or failed.

